issue.description:
Standardize theme-action success feedback so normal successful actions use the existing transient toast pattern instead of persistent inline status text, and style those toasts using semantic popover tokens.

## Context

Current save flows still render persistent success messaging at the top of the Workbench content area.

Observed examples include:

* saving an existing custom theme:
  * `Saved "Amber Dusk Custom" locally.`
* saving an edited built-in theme as a custom theme:
  * `Saved "Default Custom" as a custom theme. Default is unchanged.`

Reset already uses the newer transient toast pattern.

This ticket should unify successful theme-action feedback around that pattern.

## Goal

Replace stale/persistent inline success messaging with transient, semantic, theme-aware toast feedback.

Primary principle:

* success feedback should be temporary and non-blocking;
* errors and warnings should remain explicit and must not be suppressed;
* theme actions should feel like one coherent feedback system.

## 1. Save existing custom theme

When saving changes to an existing custom theme:

* do not render a persistent inline success message in the content area;
* show a transient toast instead.

Recommended concise copy:

**Theme saved**

Preserve the existing save behavior and persistence semantics.

## 2. Save edited built-in theme / Save As flow

When saving an edited built-in theme as a custom theme:

* preserve the existing Save As / naming flow;
* preserve built-in immutability;
* after successful creation/save, do not render a persistent inline status message;
* show a transient toast instead.

Recommended concise copy:

**Theme saved as custom theme**

Do not reintroduce the old inline message such as:

`Saved "Default Custom" as a custom theme. Default is unchanged.`

The underlying semantic behavior remains unchanged; only the success-feedback presentation changes.

## 3. Audit equivalent theme-action success messaging

Search the Workbench/app for other persistent or duplicated normal-success messages associated with theme actions.

Audit at minimum:

* Save
* Save As / custom-theme creation
* Reset
* Import
* Export
* Delete
* theme duplication/creation paths if present
* other local theme persistence actions

For each:

* retain useful error/warning/confirmation behavior;
* normalize routine success feedback to the existing transient toast system where appropriate;
* avoid duplicated feedback such as both inline status + toast for the same success event;
* do not invent new toast messages where an action currently has no need for success feedback.

This should be a systematic audit rather than a one-string removal.

## 4. Toast surface styling

Update the shared theme-action toast surface to use semantic popover tokens rather than a hard-coded/generic white surface.

Preferred semantic treatment:

* background: `popover`
* foreground: `popover-foreground`
* border: `border`
* retain existing subtle shadow/elevation

Conceptually:

`bg-popover text-popover-foreground border-border`

or the exact accepted equivalent in the current toast implementation.

Requirements:

* theme-responsive;
* coherent in light/dark mode;
* works with built-in and custom themes;
* no toast-specific color token;
* no hard-coded white background.

## 5. Reset toast consistency

The existing Reset toast should adopt the same shared popover surface treatment.

Preserve:

* title/copy;
* Undo action;
* Dismiss action;
* timing/dismiss behavior;
* reset semantics.

This ticket should not redesign Reset interaction.

## 6. Success semantics

Keep normal success toasts visually restrained.

Do not make the entire toast green or introduce a large success-colored surface.

If an existing success icon/accent pattern is already available and appropriate, it may be reused, but the primary surface remains neutral:

* `popover`
* `popover-foreground`
* `border`

## 7. Preserve important non-success feedback

Do not suppress or downgrade:

* validation errors;
* persistence errors;
* import failures;
* destructive confirmations;
* warnings;
* unsaved-state warnings;
* any message that requires user attention or action.

This ticket targets routine successful-action feedback only.

## 8. Boundaries

Do not change:

* Theme v3 schema;
* persistence format;
* Save / Save As semantics;
* built-in/custom theme rules;
* Reset semantics;
* Import/Export data contracts;
* Delete semantics;
* history/undo behavior;
* Design Command;
* mutation operations;
* public registry;
* dependencies;
* network behavior;
* public component APIs.

Prefer reusing the existing toast implementation and current shadcn/Sonner-style composition.

## 9. Verification

Verify at minimum:

* saving an existing custom theme shows no persistent inline success message;
* saving an existing custom theme shows the transient success toast;
* saving an edited built-in theme through Save As shows no persistent inline success message;
* Save As success shows the transient success toast;
* Reset still uses toast feedback and retains Undo/Dismiss behavior;
* theme-action toast surface uses semantic popover/background tokens rather than hard-coded white;
* popover styling responds correctly to built-in/custom themes;
* light/dark mode remains coherent;
* no duplicate inline + toast success feedback remains in audited theme-action paths;
* error/warning/confirmation flows remain intact;
* keyboard/focus behavior is not regressed.

## Acceptance

* Routine theme-action success feedback is standardized on transient toasts.
* Existing custom-theme Save no longer leaves persistent inline success text.
* Built-in-theme Save As success no longer leaves persistent inline success text.
* Shared toast surface uses `popover`, `popover-foreground`, and semantic border treatment or exact accepted equivalent.
* Reset uses the same shared toast surface without changing its interaction semantics.
* Equivalent stale success-message paths are audited and normalized where appropriate.
* Errors, warnings, destructive confirmations, and other important feedback remain intact.
* No Theme v3, persistence, Design Command, mutation, registry, dependency, network, or public API regression is introduced.
